{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Fort Worth & Denver City Railway and its Colorado & Southern connection north of Texline - both Burlington (CB&Q) subsidiaries - forming the Denver-Amarillo-Fort Worth-Dallas corridor, and the Texas Zephyr streamliner that ran it. Amarillo was literally founded because of this railroad. Route geometry is hand-placed waypoints through the real cities the line served, NOT from any modern Amtrak alignment, because no passenger train has run this corridor since 1967. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones; headway_min and avg_speed_kmh are estimates.", "source": "Wikipedia (Texas Zephyr, Fort Worth and Denver Railway), american-rails.com, tshaonline.org, myhighplains.com", "pulled": "20260818", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Fort Worth & Denver City / Colorado & Southern (Burlington Route)", "line": "Fort Worth & Denver City: Denver \u2013 Dallas", "id": "fwd-texas-zephyr", "color": "#8E1B3C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 68, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1888, "end": 1940, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Amarillo was founded in 1887 by businessmen looking to capitalize on the Fort Worth & Denver City Railway then being built across the Texas Panhandle; the first FW&DC passenger train reached Amarillo from Clarendon in March 1888. By 1903 three railroads served Amarillo, making it a major cattle-shipping and transportation hub. The heavyweight Colorado Special worked this Denver-Texas route for decades - the most popular rail link between Colorado and Texas. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Texas Zephyr, Fort Worth and Denver Railway), american-rails.com, tshaonline.org, myhighplains.com"]}, {"start": 1940, "end": 1967, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The streamliner era reached Amarillo Aug 23 1940 with the Texas Zephyr, run jointly by the Colorado & Southern and Fort Worth & Denver (both CB&Q subsidiaries) from Denver Union Station via Colorado Springs, Pueblo, Trinidad, Amarillo and Wichita Falls to Fort Worth and Dallas, replacing the Colorado Special. It ran Budd stainless-steel chair cars and an observation dining-lounge behind pairs of EMD E5 diesels. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Texas Zephyr, Fort Worth and Denver Railway), american-rails.com, tshaonline.org, myhighplains.com"]}, {"start": 1967, "end": 1967, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Texas Zephyr made its last run Sept 11 1967, ending passenger service on this corridor four years before Amtrak; Amarillo's remaining service was the Santa Fe's San Francisco Chief until 1971 (see santa_fe_chief.geojson). estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Texas Zephyr, Fort Worth and Denver Railway), american-rails.com, tshaonline.org, myhighplains.com"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-104.8214, 38.8339], "to": [-104.6098, 38.2544], "chord_mi": 41.76, "traced_mi": 44.68, "snap_m": [541, 37], "bridged_mi": 0